  "pattern": "two-pointers / Converge ends",
  "statement": "Given an integer array `nums` arranged in non-decreasing numerical order, return a new array containing the squares of each number, also sorted in non-decreasing order.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 100` and `-1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-1000]",
      "output": "[1000000]",
      "explanation": "Generated from the verified optimal implementation."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000]",
      "output": "[1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000, 1000000]",
      "explanation": "Generated from the verified optimal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function sortedSquares(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "After squaring, negative and positive elements each form a sorted run; merge the two runs.",
    "Use two pointers starting at the opposite ends of the sorted input.",
    "Place the larger square at the current write position (filling from the end) and advance the pointer that contributed it."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Square each element then sort (brute). The optimal exploits that the input is sorted: the negatives produce a decreasing run of squares and positives an increasing run, so the two-pointer merge produces the sorted result in one pass.",
    "why_optimal": "Brute force is O(n log n) due to the re-sort. The two-pointer merge is O(n) since each element is visited once. On 22 cases the measured times were 0.84 ms (brute) vs 1.95 ms (optimal); Python's Timsort is fast on tiny partially-sorted arrays, so the optimal only pulls ahead at larger n.",
    "pitfalls": "The input is sorted by value, not by absolute value\u2014large negative numbers dominate. Fill the result from the end (largest square first) so you never need a reversal."
  },
  "reading_links": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm"
  ],
